Subject: winemac.drv: snap a borderless display-sized window to the display origin (macOS)

A borderless window sized exactly to a display is a fullscreen game window
(Unity's -popupwindow, for instance). Some titles position it at the work area
-- below the menu bar -- rather than at the display origin, which leaves the
menu bar visible across the top and clips the same number of pixels off the
bottom of the frame.

Snap such a window to cover the display. The frame-changed event generated at
the end of setFrameFromWine: syncs the corrected origin back to the Win32 side,
so input coordinate mapping follows the window and clicks still land where the
game expects them.

The candidate display is the one containing the window's centre, not simply the
first display of a matching size: with two identically-sized displays, choosing
by size alone would teleport a window from the second to the first. A window
placed on the work area is still centred within its display's frame, so the
centre identifies it correctly.

The match is otherwise deliberately narrow -- borderless, non-empty, and within
a pixel of the display's size in both axes, but not already at its origin -- so
an ordinary borderless window that merely happens to be display-sized is not
moved unless it is also misplaced.

Split out of the DXMT winemac patch, which it shared a file with but has
nothing to do with.

diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
index 7d6d704..7487fe4 100644
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_window.m
@@ -1956,6 +1956,35 @@ - (void) setFrameFromWine:(NSRect)contentRect
            (left, bottom) in a bottom-up space. */
         [[WineApplicationController sharedController] flipRect:&contentRect];
 
+        /* A borderless window sized exactly to a display is a fullscreen game
+           window (e.g. Unity's -popupwindow).  Some apps position it at the
+           work area (below the menu bar) instead of the display origin, which
+           leaves the menu bar visible and clips the bottom.  Snap it to cover
+           the display; the frame-changed event generated at the end of this
+           method syncs the new origin back to the Win32 side, so input mapping
+           stays correct. */
+        if (!([self styleMask] & NSWindowStyleMaskTitled) && !NSIsEmptyRect(contentRect))
+        {
+            NSPoint center = NSMakePoint(NSMidX(contentRect), NSMidY(contentRect));
+
+            for (NSScreen* snapScreen in [NSScreen screens])
+            {
+                NSRect sf = [snapScreen frame];
+
+                /* Match the display the window is actually on, not merely the
+                   first one of its size: with two identical displays, choosing
+                   by size alone teleports a window from the second to the
+                   first.  A window sitting on the work area is still centred
+                   within its display's frame, so the centre identifies it. */
+                if (!NSPointInRect(center, sf)) continue;
+                if (fabs(contentRect.size.width - sf.size.width) < 1.0 &&
+                    fabs(contentRect.size.height - sf.size.height) < 1.0 &&
+                    !NSEqualPoints(contentRect.origin, sf.origin))
+                    contentRect.origin = sf.origin;
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+
         /* The back end is establishing a new window size and position.  It's
            not interested in any stale events regarding those that may be sitting
            in the queue. */
